course: dictionary
lesson: 8524
title: Sauropod: the giant plant-eater
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sauropod was a huge, plant-eating dinosaur. You can work out its meaning from the words around it: «Sauropods were long-necked plant-eating dinosaurs and the biggest animals ever to walk on land.» Long-necked, plant-eating and biggest all tell you that a sauropod was a very large animal that ate plants.
explain: «Diplodocus was about 25 metres long, mostly neck and tail.» Diplodocus is one kind of sauropod, so the sentence shows you what a sauropod could look like. Notice that the word can be a noun, as in «a sauropod», or an adjective, as in «sauropod footprints».
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «A huge, plant-eating dinosaur with a long neck needed a big, powerful heart to pump blood all the way up to its head.» It still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: A sauropod was a huge, plant-eating dinosaur with a very long neck, a long tail, a small head and four thick legs like pillars.
fact: Diplodocus, Brachiosaurus and Argentinosaurus were all sauropods.
fact: Sauropods were the biggest animals ever to walk on land; some were longer than three buses.
fact: Their long necks let them reach leaves high up, or sweep over a wide area without walking.
fact: The name sauropod means «lizard foot».
exercise: What is a sauropod? | A small, meat-eating bird | A fish that lives in deep seas | *A huge, plant-eating dinosaur with a long neck | A kind of bus
exercise: The phrase «a herd of sauropods» has a word for a group of animals. Which word is it? | sauropods | *herd | a | of
exercise: What does the name sauropod mean? | Giant bird | Plant eater | *Lizard foot | Long neck
test: Why could a sauropod reach leaves high up? | Because it climbed trees with its tail | Because it could fly | *Because its long neck reached high | Because its head was huge
test: In the phrase «sauropod footprints», what kind of word is sauropod? | Noun | Adverb | Verb | *Adjective
exam: Which of these is a sauropod? | A whale | A giraffe | *Brachiosaurus | A bird
